@startuml c_created
!include _style.puml
title C1–C5 — tickets created on booking: a row exists only once sold

entity "event" as e {
  * event_id : BIGINT <<PK>>
  --
  * band_id : BIGINT <<FK>>
  * capacity : INT
  * description : TEXT
  <back:#D8F0DE>* seats_sold : INT  (C4 only, CHECK 0..capacity)</back>
}
entity "ticket  (sold seats only)" as t {
  * ticket_id : BIGINT <<PK>>
  --
  * event_id : BIGINT <<FK>>
  seat_no : INT  (NOT NULL in C5)
  * customer_id : BIGINT
  <back:#FFF3CD>event_capacity : INT  (C5 only)</back>
  --
  C5: UNIQUE (event_id, seat_no)
  C5: CHECK (seat_no BETWEEN 1 AND event_capacity)
}
e ||--o{ t

note right of t
  **C1 count (RC)** <back:#F5D0D0> NEGATIVE CONTROL </back>
  seats_left = capacity − COUNT(tickets)
  if > 0: INSERT ticket
  Two buyers both see the last seat free.

  **C2 count (SER)** — identical SQL, SERIALIZABLE;
  the engine aborts one of the two (40001), retry

  **C3 count+lock**
  SELECT … FROM event **FOR UPDATE**, then C1's count
  — a per-event critical section around an O(sold) count

  **C4 counter**
  UPDATE event SET seats_sold = seats_sold + 1
   WHERE event_id=$ **AND seats_sold < capacity**
  no row → sold out; else INSERT ticket

  **C5 seat-unique** (autocommit, no lock)
  n = MAX(seat_no); INSERT seat n+1 **ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING**
  0 rows → another buyer took n+1, retry
  Refunds leave holes that n+1 never refills.
end note
@enduml
